      "repair_reason": "- [P1] The mechanical repair is clear, but an OpenShell owner must first approve the user-visible 100 MiB compatibility boundary.; Needs attention: The hardening goal is sound, but the patch creates a parallel security-sensitive bounded-read implementation instead of using the established fs-safe contract.; Review finding: [P1] Reuse the bounded fs-safe read contract — extensions/openshell/src/fs-bridge.ts:38-62 (sha=eb88e695bd2dc9ffcb61fff84b3c83d0676a70a6)",
      "review_summary": "The PR replaces OpenShell’s unbounded sandbox file-handle read with an asynchronous 100 MiB chunked-read cap in `extensions/openshell/src/fs-bridge.ts`.\n\nPR surface: Source +30. Total +30 across 1 file.\n\nReproducibility: no. live reproduction was provided or run here, but source inspection gives a high-confidence path: current `main` uses an unbounded `FileHandle.readFile()` after opening the sandbox-approved path. ([raw.githubusercontent.com](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openclaw/openclaw/main/extensions/openshell/src/fs-bridge.ts))",
      "review_followup": "- [P1] The mechanical repair is clear, but an OpenShell owner must first approve the user-visible 100 MiB compatibility boundary.",
